Here's pdf_search.py. It uses PyMuPDF, which is fast enough for PDFs with thousands of pages.

Setup

pip install pymupdf rapidfuzz pandas openpyxl
pip install sentence-transformers   # only for "ideas" search

Interactive use: run python pdf_search.py bigfile.pdf and type searches one after another. The PDF loads once, so later searches come back almost instantly.

Plain text finds an exact word or phrase, even when the phrase is split across a line break in the PDF.
~ medicaton recrd is fuzzy search, which tolerates typos and OCR errors.
re: \d{2}:\d{2} is a regex search, for things like timestamps, case numbers or dates.
? the record was changed after the patient died is an "ideas" search. It finds sentences with the same meaning even if none of those words appear in them.
:save hits.xlsx exports every hit with its file, page and surrounding context, plus a summary sheet.

Batch use: search a whole folder against a term list and save the results:

python pdf_search.py C:\Cases\Smith --recursive --terms terms.txt -o hits.xlsx

terms.txt has one term per line, which makes it easy to keep a standard term list for each case.

What I tested and what to know:

I tested exact, fuzzy and regex search here. I couldn't test the "ideas" mode, because downloading its model is blocked in my sandbox.
The first "ideas" search downloads a roughly 90 MB model one time. After that it runs fully offline, so documents never leave your machine.
That mode caches each PDF's index on disk, so a large file is processed only once.
Scanned PDFs have no text to search. The script flags them, and running ocrmypdf in.pdf out.pdf first fixes that.
